  "Number": 12,
  "Question": "If \\(g(x)=1-x^2\\) and \\(f(g(x))=\\frac{1-x^2}{x^2}\\) when \\(x\\ne0,\\) then \\(f(\\frac{1}{2})\\) equals",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(\\frac{3}{4}\\)",
    "B": "\\(1\\)",
    "C": "\\(3\\)",
    "D": "\\(\\frac{\\sqrt2}{2}\\)",
    "E": "\\(\\sqrt2\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "B",
  "Solution": "To find \\(f(\\tfrac12)\\), choose an input \\(x\\) for which \\(g(x)=\\tfrac12\\). Since \\(g(x)=1-x^2\\), we want \\(x^2=\\tfrac12\\). One permissible choice is \\(x=1/\\sqrt2\\), which is nonzero.\n\nSubstituting this input into the given identity yields\n\\[\nf\\left(\\frac12\\right)\n=\\frac{1-\\frac12}{\\frac12}=1.\n\\]\nThus the answer is \\(1\\), choice B.",
